<!-- ===================== THE MAP ===================== -->
<section class="sec" id="map">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">00</span>
      <h2>The argument in one screen</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom:1.4rem">
      <p>
        One setup, three pillars, one payoff. Each pillar is a thing we can show that most
        RISC-V talks cannot: a working large model, a measured kernel contribution, and the
        instrument that made both possible.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="map">
      <div class="map-row">
        <div class="map-k">Setup</div>
        <div class="map-v">Edge memory is the constraint. 1-bit quantization fits a 27B model in
          3.8 GB, but it deletes the multiply, so <b>the kernel becomes a data-layout and ISA
          problem</b>. Upstream llama.cpp answers it badly.</div>
        <div class="map-n">2 min</div>
      </div>
      <div class="map-row pillar">
        <div class="map-k">Pillar 1<br>Capability</div>
        <div class="map-v"><b>Bonsai 4B, 8B and 27B all run on one AX46MPV hart.</b> Whole
          models, not slices, producing correct text on a core that does not exist in silicon
          yet. 27B is 3.8 GB of 1-bit weights.</div>
        <div class="map-n">4 min</div>
      </div>
      <div class="map-row pillar">
        <div class="map-k">Pillar 2<br>Contribution</div>
        <div class="map-v"><b>Four changes to the llama.cpp Q1_0 decode path.</b> Three are
          portable RVV and upstreamable; one uses a stock Andes V5 instruction. 11.9x combined,
          bit-identical output.</div>
        <div class="map-n">12 min</div>
      </div>
      <div class="map-row pillar">
        <div class="map-k">Pillar 3<br>Product</div>
        <div class="map-v"><b>andesim: one simulator, three modes.</b> Functional for bring-up,
          hybrid for exact per-kernel cycles, cycle-accurate from reset for the whole system. The
          work in Pillar 2 is what the tool is for.</div>
        <div class="map-n">8 min</div>
      </div>
      <div class="map-row">
        <div class="map-k">Payoff</div>
        <div class="map-v">A 27B-class model on an edge core, with the software and the
          measurement product both in place <b>before tape-out</b>. Plus the negative results,
          which are what tell you where not to spend engineering.</div>
        <div class="map-n">3 min</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 01 OPENING ===================== -->
<section class="sec" id="open">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">01</span>
      <h2>Opening: why 1-bit, why RISC-V, why now</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ob">
      <div class="h"><h3>The constraint is memory, not math</h3><span class="slide">2 slides</span></div>
      <div class="field job"><div class="fk">Job</div>
        <p>Earn the room's attention in 90 seconds and set up why the rest is a kernel talk, not
          a model talk.</p></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">Claim</div>
        <p>1-bit quantization is what puts a 27B model on an edge device. But it removes the
          multiply, so all the remaining cost is layout and instruction issue - which is exactly
          where a wide RVV core wins or loses.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Evidence I have</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> Q1_0 block: 128 weights in 16 bytes + one fp16 scale</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> Bonsai-27B fits 3.8 GB on disk, 64 layers, 497 MUL_MAT per token</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> upstream kernel uses <b>32 of 128</b> int8 lanes at VLEN=1024</li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field gap"><div class="fk">Gap</div>
        <p>Do we open with the model output on screen (a live/recorded token stream) or with the
          lane-utilization picture? The first is more visceral, the second sets up the technical
          spine faster.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 02 PILLAR 1 ===================== -->
<section class="sec" id="p1">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">02</span>
      <h2>Pillar 1: we run a 27B model on one core</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ob">
      <div class="h"><h3>Three models, all producing correct text</h3><span class="slide">3 slides</span></div>
      <div class="field job"><div class="fk">Job</div>
        <p>Establish that this is real and complete, before any optimization numbers. Nobody
          believes the speedups if they doubt the workload.</p></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">Claim</div>
        <p>Bonsai 4B, 8B and 27B each run end to end on a single AX46MPV hart at VLEN=1024, with
          the optimized kernel active, and produce coherent output.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Evidence I have - all three confirmed from run logs</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><span class="have">logged</span> 4B and 8B and 27B all print "The capital of France is Paris."</li>
          <li><span class="have">logged</span> every run single-threaded, <code>RVV_VLEN</code> 128 bytes = 1024 bits</li>
          <li><span class="have">logged</span> 27B: qwen35, 64 layers, 3.8 GB, 497 MUL_MAT nodes per token, 8 tokens in 15.5 min</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> 27B needed &gt;2 GiB guest RAM - now up to 16 GiB via a high RAM base</li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">The distinction this slide MUST make</div>
        <p>Correctness comes from the functional model. Cycles come from RTL. They are two
          different runs and the talk has to say so.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">How to say it</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><b>Functional</b> - full model, all 497 nodes, every token. Proves it runs and is correct. No timing meaning.</li>
          <li><b>RTL</b> - one kernel at a time, exact cycles. Proves speed. Cannot run a whole token.</li>
          <li>Why not both at once: at ~3.8 kHz and ~3.9 G cycles per 27B token, <b>one token on RTL would take 12 days</b></li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Strong sub-story, optional</div>
        <p style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--ink-2)">We found and fixed a real RVV correctness
          bug getting here: an uninitialized tail-agnostic accumulator in
          <code>ggml_vec_dot_f32</code> produced ~1e19 dot products on RISC-V only. Upstream
          contribution and a good "RISC-V software is still young" moment - costs 2 minutes.</p></div>
      <div class="field gap"><div class="fk">Gap</div>
        <p>Include the correctness-bug story or cut it for time? And can we show the actual
          generated text on a slide, or is the model output sensitive?</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 03 PILLAR 2 ===================== -->
<section class="sec" id="p2">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">03</span>
      <h2>Pillar 2: four changes to the llama.cpp decode path</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom:.4rem">
      <p>
        This is the core of the talk and gets the most time. Each change follows the same
        three-beat shape: <em>what was the bottleneck</em>, <em>what we changed</em>,
        <em>what it bought</em> - so the audience learns a method, not four tricks.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw">
      <table>
        <caption>Measured decode / gemv ladder, cycles, one MUL_MAT node per model</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What changed</th><th>Portable?</th><th>4B</th><th>8B</th><th>27B</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Upstream</td><td>ggml_vec_dot_q1_0_q8_0</td><td>-</td><td>4,476,673</td><td>7,278,168</td><td>54,314,202</td></tr>
          <tr><td>+ Repack</td><td>64 rows into the lanes</td><td>portable RVV</td><td>2,144,213</td><td>3,475,147</td><td>25,629,722</td></tr>
          <tr><td>+ Prefetch</td><td>zicbop weight stream</td><td>portable RVV</td><td>1,389,236</td><td>2,218,540</td><td>16,317,304</td></tr>
          <tr><td>+ vd4dots</td><td>Andes V5 XAndesVDot</td><td>Andes V5</td><td>362,799</td><td>648,246</td><td>4,578,346</td></tr>
          <tr class="hi"><td>Total</td><td>same numerics throughout</td><td></td><td>12.34x</td><td>11.23x</td><td>11.86x</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="font-family:var(--f-mono);font-size:.66rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem">
      Nodes: 4B / 8B <code>blk.7.attn_v</code>, 27B <code>blk.6.attn_gate</code>. Each column is
      one node measured four times, not a whole-token figure.
    </p>

    <div class="ob">
      <div class="h"><h3>The four changes, one slide each</h3><span class="slide">6 slides</span></div>
      <div class="field job"><div class="fk">Job</div>
        <p>Show a repeatable optimization method and make clear which parts any RISC-V vendor can
          take and which part is ours.</p></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">Claim</div>
        <p>Three portable RVV changes get 3.3x. The Andes vd4dot instruction gets the remaining
          3.6x on top - and it is a stock vendor instruction, not custom silicon.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Evidence I have</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> 01 repack - 64 rows per lane group, zero horizontal reductions, 2.09-2.12x</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> 02 prefetch - 18 cache lines ahead, 1.54x on top, distance swept</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> 03 vd4dots - 256 MACs per instruction, 3.04x on top</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> 04 vtype churn - 4.00 to 0.94 vsetvli per dot, 1.26x, no math changed</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> bit-identical output at every stage, 13 shape cases</li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field gap"><div class="fk">Gap</div>
        <p>Four changes in 12 minutes is tight. Proposal: give repack and vd4dots a full slide
          each, compress prefetch and the vtype fix into one shared "and then we chased the
          remaining stalls" slide. Confirm?</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="ob">
      <div class="h"><h3>The result generalizes - the strongest slide in the deck</h3><span class="slide">1 slide</span></div>
      <div class="field job"><div class="fk">Job</div>
        <p>Pre-empt "you tuned one kernel on one shape". This is the credibility slide.</p></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">Claim</div>
        <p>Cost per weight is constant across a 7x range of model size. The optimization is a
          property of the layout, not of a tuned shape.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Evidence I have</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> cycles per element, 4B / 8B / 27B:</li>
          <li>&nbsp;&nbsp;upstream <b>1.71 / 1.74 / 1.73</b></li>
          <li>&nbsp;&nbsp;repack <b>0.82 / 0.83 / 0.81</b></li>
          <li>&nbsp;&nbsp;prefetch <b>0.264 / 0.264 / 0.259</b></li>
          <li>&nbsp;&nbsp;vd4dots <b>0.138 / 0.155 / 0.146</b></li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> totals 12.34x / 11.23x / 11.86x</li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field gap"><div class="fk">Gap</div>
        <p>None. This data is complete and is the best evidence we have.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 04 PILLAR 3 ===================== -->
<section class="sec" id="p3">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">04</span>
      <h2>Pillar 3: andesim, one simulator with three modes</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom:.4rem">
      <p>
        This pillar is the product. The frame is a <em>fidelity dial</em>: the same binary, the
        same engine definition, three points on a speed-versus-exactness curve. Pillar 2 is the
        proof that the middle setting is the one that changes how you work.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw">
      <table>
        <caption>The three modes - the product slide</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Mode</th><th>What runs</th><th>Timing fidelity</th><th>Measured on 27B</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>fast</td><td>QEMU only, functional</td><td>none</td><td>8 tokens / 15.5 min</td><td>bring-up, correctness</td></tr>
          <tr class="hi"><td>hybrid</td><td>QEMU fast-forwards, RTL measures the ROI</td><td>exact, for the ROI</td><td>~25 min per kernel</td><td>kernel optimization</td></tr>
          <tr><td>cycle</td><td>one cycle-accurate engine, from reset</td><td>exact, everything</td><td>not run</td><td>full-system, boot</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="ob">
      <div class="h"><h3>Why the middle mode exists</h3><span class="slide">2 slides</span></div>
      <div class="field job"><div class="fk">Job</div>
        <p>Make the audience feel the gap that hybrid fills, using our own workload as the
          worked example. Without this, hybrid sounds like a convenience feature.</p></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">Claim</div>
        <p>fast has no timing. cycle is exact but cannot reach a kernel that sits 9 minutes of
          model loading into a 497-node graph. hybrid fast-forwards through the boring part and
          spends RTL only where you are looking.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Evidence I have</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> RTL runs ~3.8 kHz. A 27B token is ~3.9 G cycles, so <b>one token = 12 days</b></li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> hybrid splits it: ~5.6 min functional leg, then ~21 min RTL for a 4.8M-cycle node</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> repeatable to the cycle - 6 of 7 re-runs bit-identical across a full engine rebuild</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> any node addressable by index, all 497 of them</li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field gap"><div class="fk">Gap</div>
        <p>We have no measured <code>cycle</code>-mode number to put in that table. Either run one
          small workload from reset to fill the cell, or present cycle mode by capability only.
          A real number would be much stronger - want me to get one?</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="ob">
      <div class="h"><h3>Two features worth showing on screen</h3><span class="slide">2 slides</span></div>
      <div class="field job"><div class="fk">Job</div>
        <p>Give the product pillar something visual. Tables do not sell a tool; a pipeline trace
          does.</p></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">Claim</div>
        <p>andesim does not ask you to trust the handoff, and it will show you the pipeline.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Evidence I have</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><b>--verify</b> cross-checks a hybrid run against a QEMU oracle, so the fast-forward
            is validated rather than assumed. This is the answer to the obvious audience
            question.</li>
          <li><b>--kanata</b> writes a pipeline trace of the ROI for Konata.
            <span class="have">confirmed working</span> - 651 MB trace for one kernel. A
            before/after pipeline view of the same matmul would be the strongest single visual in
            the deck.</li>
          <li>Multi-engine from one CLI: <code>ax46mpv_fpga_l3</code>, <code>ax45mpv_premium</code>
            and others differ only by <code>--engine</code></li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field gap"><div class="fk">Gap</div>
        <p>Do you want me to actually generate the before/after Konata screenshots? That is a
          real time cost but it would carry the whole pillar.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="ob">
      <div class="h"><h3>What the loop caught: three negative results</h3><span class="slide">1 slide</span></div>
      <div class="field job"><div class="fk">Job</div>
        <p>This is the differentiator, and it is the strongest sales argument the product has.
          Anyone can show a speedup chart; showing what you correctly did <em>not</em> build is
          what makes the tool worth buying.</p></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">Claim</div>
        <p>The same loop that found the wins killed three plausible ideas - and in one case a
          cheaper tool had ranked the answer exactly backwards.</p></div>
      <div class="field ev"><div class="fk">Evidence I have</div>
        <ul class="ev-list">
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> the QEMU instruction-count proxy <b>inverted</b> a result: a stall-removal
            restructure read as a 31% regression (57.5us to 75.5us) because TCG charges per vector
            instruction and models no stalls. That feature was dropped on the proxy's word.</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> more scratch registers: <b>fewer</b> instructions per dot, 4.5% <b>slower</b>, reverted</li>
          <li><span class="have">measured</span> a compiler-contracted stride-0 load cost <b>18x</b>, silently, with correct output</li>
        </ul></div>
      <div class="field claim"><div class="fk">The line that lands</div>
        <p>All three look right on paper and two of them look right under a fast simulator. Only
          cycle-accurate measurement ranks them correctly.</p></div>
      <div class="field gap"><div class="fk">Gap</div>
        <p>None blocking. Confirm you are happy naming QEMU as the tool that got it backwards -
          it is a fair and widely understood point, but it is a public comparison.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

